BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:surf-artist-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260926T163109Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sanford Underground Research Facility: Artist-in-Residenc
 e Program 2027 (Lead\, South Dakota)
DESCRIPTION:The SURF Artist-in-Residence program invites artists to create 
 work inspired by the Sanford Underground Research Facility in Lead\, South
  Dakota. At 4\,850 feet deep\, SURF is the deepest underground laboratory 
 in the United States\, housed in the former Homestake Gold Mine\, and host
 s experiments in physics\, biology\, geology and engineering. The residenc
 y is modeled after programs run by the National Park Service\, CERN and Fe
 rmilab\, and aims to create awareness of the science and encourage interdi
 sciplinary work. STRUCTURE: a four-week on-site visit in summer 2027 (betw
 een 22 May and 31 July\, timed to the artist's schedule) with studio time\
 , guided underground visits\, tours of Lead and the surrounding Black Hill
 s sites\, and dinners with scientists\; then a one-week visit in October 2
 027 to install an art exhibition\, give up to two lectures based on the pr
 oposal\, and run promotional or outreach events in the local community (wh
 ich may include schools\, universities\, interviews or documentation of th
 e residency). FUNDING: a USD 5\,000 stipend paid in two installments\; the
  artist may apply for up to USD 5\,000 in additional funds for creating th
 e artwork\; transportation\, housing and per diem for both visits\; and re
 turn shipping covered up to USD 7\,000. CONDITIONS: each artist donates at
  least one artwork\, selected by a SURF representative\, to SURF's permane
 nt art collection\, and is responsible for installation and de-installatio
 n in coordination with SURF and the exhibition space. A timeline and expec
 tations are agreed in an MOU before the residency begins. PHYSICAL REQUIRE
 MENTS: working underground requires being comfortable in dark spaces\, fol
 lowing all safety protocols\, walking on uneven surfaces for at least a mi
 le at a time\, and negotiating wet\, slippery conditions and an elevator r
 ide in the dark\; safety training and loaned personal protective equipment
  are mandatory\, and a SURF guide accompanies the artist at all times. DEA
 DLINE: 1 November 2026\, via the SURF AiR application portal.\n\nOrganisat
 ion: Sanford Underground Research Facility (SURF)\nLocation: Sanford Under
 ground Research Facility\, Lead\, South Dakota\, USA\, in the Black Hills.
  The call does not state a residency or nationality restriction\; travel\,
  housing and per diem for both visits are covered.\nAward: USD 5\,000 stip
 end paid in two installments\, plus up to USD 5\,000 in additional funds t
 he artist may apply for to create the artwork. Transportation (airfare\, r
 ental car or mileage)\, housing and per diem are covered for both visits\;
  a studio space and guided surface and underground tours are provided\; re
 turn shipping of the exhibition is covered up to USD 7\,000.\nDuration: Fo
 ur-week on-site residency in summer 2027 (scheduled between 22 May and 31 
 July in coordination with SURF)\, plus a one-week exhibition installation 
 and guest-lecture visit in October 2027.\n\nMore: https://sanfordlab.org/p
 rograms/SURFAiR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sanfordlab.org/programs/SURFAiR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Sanford Underground Research Facility\, Lead\, South Dakota\, USA\
 , in the Black Hills. The call does not state a residency or nationality r
 estriction\; travel\, housing and per diem for both visits are covered.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260926T163109Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:simons-foundation-open-interval-turbulence-2026@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260926T163109Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:Deadline: Open Interval: An Artist-Scientist Exploration of Turbule
 nce
DESCRIPTION:Open Interval is a Simons Foundation program that supports trio
 s consisting of an artist\, a scientist\, and a representative from a 501(
 c)(3) arts organization to collaborate around a shared scientific theme. T
 he current cycle centers on the theme of turbulence\, a phenomenon at the 
 edge of chaos and beauty. Awarded trios receive USD 30\,000 to support a s
 ustained period of thought partnership and exploration rather than the pro
 duction of finished works or exhibitions. The program runs from April to D
 ecember 2027 with a touchstone meeting in May 2027 and possible cohort con
 venings. Trio members must be regionally co-located anywhere in the United
  States so they can collaborate in person. The scientist should hold a Ph.
 D. and be conducting basic research\, with priority given to fields such a
 s astrophysics\, neuroscience\, quantum physics\, biology\, or mathematics
 . The application portal opens September 9\, 2026\, and all three trio mem
 bers must submit for the application to be complete.\n\nOrganisation: Simo
 ns Foundation\nLocation: United States (trio must be regionally co-located
 )\nAward: USD 30\,000 per awarded trio\nDuration: April to December 2027 (
 several months of collaboration)\n\nMore: https://www.simonsfoundation.org
 /2026/06/01/coming-soon-open-interval-an-artist-scientist-exploration-of-t
 urbulence/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.simonsfoundation.org/2026/06/01/coming-soon-open-interval-a
 n-artist-scientist-exploration-of-turbulence/?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States (trio must be regionally co-located)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kluge-fellowships-library-of-congress-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260926T163109Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kluge Fellowships at the Library of Congress 2027
DESCRIPTION:The John W. Kluge Center awards twelve residential fellowships 
 a year for humanistic and social science research that draws on the Librar
 y of Congress collections\, with interdisciplinary and cross-cultural proj
 ects particularly welcome. Fellows get four to eleven months in residence 
 at a stipend of USD 5\,000 per month\, work and discussion space in the Je
 fferson Building\, and access to the Library's specialist staff\, the worl
 d's largest law library\, extensive multilingual books and periodicals\, a
 nd deep special collections of manuscripts\, maps\, music\, films\, record
 ed sound\, prints and photographs\, alongside the wider scholarly communit
 y in Washington. The Center was established in 2000 through a USD 60 milli
 on endowment from John W. Kluge. ELIGIBILITY: scholars who received a term
 inal advanced degree (the highest degree available in the discipline\, usu
 ally a PhD\, JD or international equivalent) within the past seven years\,
  in the humanities\, social sciences\, or a professional field such as arc
 hitecture or law. The degree must be in hand at the time of submission\, a
 nd full-time students are therefore out of scope. Open to U.S. citizens an
 d foreign nationals\; selected foreign nationals are assisted in obtaining
  a Library-sponsored J-1 visa\, though holders of H-1B visas are not eligi
 ble. APPLICATION: through the Library's online portal\, opening 15 April 2
 026 and closing 11:59pm on 15 September 2026. Materials required are an ap
 plication form in English\, a CV (max 2 pages)\, a 500-word research abstr
 act\, a one-page statement on how the Library's collections will serve the
  work\, a project proposal with work plan (max 3 pages)\, a bibliography (
 max 3 pages)\, and two reference letters from referees who have read the p
 roposal. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: John W. Kluge Center\, Library of Congre
 ss\nLocation: Washington\, DC\, United States (in residence at the Thomas 
 Jefferson Building\, 101 Independence Ave SE). Open to applicants of any n
 ationality: U.S. citizens and foreign nationals alike\, with J-1 exchange 
 visitor visa sponsorship provided for those who need it.\nAward: Stipend o
 f USD 5\,000 per month for a residency of four to eleven months\, so USD 2
 0\,000 to USD 55\,000 depending on the tenure awarded. Twelve Kluge Fellow
 ships are awarded each year. Stipends are paid monthly by electronic trans
 fer to a U.S. bank account. No application fee\, no participation fee.\nDu
 ration: Four to eleven months in residence\; fellows may take up residence
  at any point in the 18-month window after receiving the fellowship letter
 \n\nMore: https://www.loc.gov/programs/john-w-kluge-center/chairs-fellowsh
 ips/fellowships/kluge-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.loc.gov/programs/john-w-kluge-center/chairs-fellowships/fel
 lowships/kluge-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, DC\, United States (in residence at the Thomas Jeffer
 son Building\, 101 Independence Ave SE). Open to applicants of any nationa
 lity: U.S. citizens and foreign nationals alike\, with J-1 exchange visito
 r visa sponsorship provided for those who need it.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-public-entrepreneur-in-residence-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260926T163109Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: New_ Public Entrepreneur in Residence\, Pilot Cohort
DESCRIPTION:New_ Public is piloting a virtual Entrepreneur in Residence pro
 gramme for solo founders building alternatives to the mainstream social pl
 atforms. Its framing: people are turning away from algorithmic social medi
 a\, AI bots are taking over the big platforms\, and users are retreating i
 nto private closed spaces\, which leaves an opening for builders working o
 n a new information ecosystem rather than the status quo. The residency su
 pplies research\, experts\, connections and tools\; the founder brings the
  vision and experience. TWO FOCUS AREAS: humans connecting to humans\, mea
 ning products that bring people into meaningful contact online (forums\, m
 essaging apps\, groups\, microblogging\, in-person meetups)\, including AI
 -powered platforms and tools as long as they connect people to people rath
 er than people to bots\; and local and civic information environments\, me
 aning tools for distributing local news\, building community knowledge and
  facilitating collective sensemaking\, so people can get trustworthy infor
 mation about their communities. WHAT RESIDENTS DO: use self-directed time 
 to prototype ideas\, test assumptions and gain clarity\; participate in re
 gular check-ins\, engage with the other resident and the wider team\, and 
 take candid feedback on product and strategy\; engage seriously with go-to
 -market questions about who the user is\, how they find you\, and the path
  to sustainability\; and help shape the programme itself\, since the pilot
  cohort's feedback directly informs how the EIR programme runs going forwa
 rd. WHO THEY WANT: entrepreneurial founders ready to build their next thin
 g within the focus areas\, with a track record of or clear drive toward wo
 rk that builds social trust\, connection and belonging\; strong product th
 inkers with good judgment\, a relentless focus on user needs\, and opennes
 s to coaching on mission-aligned business models. An early idea is enough\
 , no working product required. The pilot is open to founders with somethin
 g else going on alongside\, though the full programme will most likely be 
 full time. APPLICATION PROCESS: submit the written application online\; sh
 ortlisted applicants submit a short video by 18 September 2026 covering ba
 ckground\, motivation and a quick pitch\; full applications and videos go 
 to the advisory board\, with offers made in early October ahead of the kic
 koff. FUTURE COHORTS: New_ Public plans to open the next cohort to the UK 
 and EU from spring 2027. Deadline 11:59pm Pacific on Wednesday 9 September
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: New_ Public\nLocation: Remote\, with an in-person 
 kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (19 to 22 October 2026). Open to app
 licants based in the United States or Canada.\nAward: Stipend of USD 22\,5
 00\, paid in three monthly installments across the residency\, each contin
 gent on continued active participation. Plus USD 1\,500 toward an AI tool 
 of the resident's choice for coding\, prototyping or design. Two residents
  are selected in the pilot cohort. Also provided: hands-on product and str
 ategy support from senior New_ Public staff and outside experts\; access t
 o New_ Public's five years of original research on moderation\, governance
 \, community stewardship and platform design\, plus direct access to the r
 esearchers behind it\; and introductions across New_ Public's network of m
 ajor platforms\, startups\, university researchers and funders for hiring\
 , fundraising\, early user testing and community partnerships. Residents m
 ust submit a Form W-9 or W-8BEN to receive the stipend. No application fee
 .\nDuration: Three months\, from the week of 19 October 2026 to 29 January
  2027\, run remotely apart from the opening retreat\n\nMore: https://newpu
 blic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://newpublic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, with an in-person kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (
 19 to 22 October 2026). Open to applicants based in the United States or C
 anada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mats-residency-program-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260926T163109Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: MATS Residency Program: 6-24 Month Funded AI Safety Resea
 rch (Winter 2027 intake)
DESCRIPTION:The MATS Residency\, announced in August 2026 as a new track al
 ongside the seasonal MATS fellowship\, funds 6 to 24 months of high-indepe
 ndence AI safety research for experienced researchers\, whether already in
  AI safety or moving in from adjacent fields. COMPENSATION: USD 155\,000 t
 o 285\,000 per year gross depending on location\, seniority and experience
 \, plus insurance\, retirement plan\, PTO (17 days US / 20 days UK)\, conf
 erence funding\, catered meals\, and up to USD 25\,000 in relocation suppo
 rt. ELIGIBILITY: at least one substantial piece of research-caliber work\;
  credentials are not required. Full-time (part-time case by case for acade
 mics on sabbatical)\, in person at MATS offices in Berkeley\, London\, or 
 Washington DC\, with visa support where required. TIMELINE: the Winter 202
 7 intake window runs 25 August to 31 October 2026 (AoE)\, with decisions b
 y end of November and interviews plus a paid work test in the first week o
 f December\; three intake windows per year.\n\nOrganisation: MATS (ML Alig
 nment and Theory Scholars)\nLocation: In-person at MATS offices in Berkele
 y\, London\, or Washington DC\; visa support provided where required. For 
 experienced researchers with at least one substantial piece of research-ca
 liber work.\nAward: USD 155\,000 to 285\,000 per year gross (set by locati
 on\, seniority\, and experience)\, plus medical/dental/vision/life insuran
 ce\, retirement plan\, paid time off\, conference funding\, catered meals\
 , and up to USD 25\,000 relocation support. No application fee.\nDuration:
  6 to 24 months with rolling start dates\; three intake windows per year.\
 n\nMore: https://www.matsprogram.org/residency?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.matsprogram.org/residency?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In-person at MATS offices in Berkeley\, London\, or Washington DC\
 ; visa support provided where required. For experienced researchers with a
 t least one substantial piece of research-caliber work.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:moha-cats-plus-residency-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260926T163109Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: MoHA: CATS+ Residency 2026 (Austin)
DESCRIPTION:CATS+ (Collaborative Art + Technology Situation) is a local res
 idency program at The Museum of Human Achievement in Austin for artists an
 d tech wizards\, grounded in hands-on experimentation\, critical thought a
 nd community-building in media art and live AV production. Cohorts take pa
 rt in weekly gatherings to learn and share on a broad range of resident-dr
 iven topics\, build troubleshooting and collaboration skills\, co-create p
 rojects\, and contribute work to MoHA's media art exhibition during the Au
 stin Studio Tour in November and/or MoHA's Holiday Show in December. WHAT 
 YOU GET: a USD 1\,500 honorarium and access to MoHA equipment and workspac
 es. WHO IS ELIGIBLE: applicants of all skill levels and fields are welcome
 \, especially those experiencing barriers to media and tech community\, kn
 owledge and tools\; must be in the Austin area\, 18 or older\, not a full-
 time student and not a CATS+ alum. DEADLINE: 1 October 2026. Info session 
 at MoHA on 28 September 2026\, 7 to 9pm (RSVP via the application page). T
 O APPLY: via the application and info page on the MoHA wiki.\n\nOrganisati
 on: The Museum of Human Achievement (MoHA)\nLocation: Austin\, Texas. Loca
 l residency: applicants must be based in the Austin area.\nAward: USD 1\,5
 00 honorarium\, plus access to MoHA equipment and workspaces.\nDuration: C
 ohort residency with weekly gatherings at MoHA\, culminating in work for M
 oHA's media art exhibition during the Austin Studio Tour in November and/o
 r MoHA's Holiday Show in December\n\nMore: https://moha.wiki/CATS%2B_Resid
 ency_Application_2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=ref
 erral
URL:https://moha.wiki/CATS%2B_Residency_Application_2026?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Austin\, Texas. Local residency: applicants must be based in the A
 ustin area.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:wave-farm-research-production-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260926T163109Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261201
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261202
SUMMARY:Deadline: Wave Farm: 2027 Research and Production Program (Transmis
 sion Art Residencies and Radio Art Fellowships\, NY)
DESCRIPTION:Wave Farm\, the Warhol Foundation-backed transmission arts cent
 er in Acra\, NY\, opens one application covering all its 2027 opportunitie
 s: the Transmission Art Residency\, the Spatial Sound Residency\, and two 
 Radio Art Fellowship tracks (Research\, and Community Engagement) connecte
 d to Wave Farm's WGXC radio station. WHAT YOU GET: a USD 1\,000 stipend fo
 r each 10-day residency and USD 2\,000 for each two-month fellowship\, wit
 h access to Wave Farm's research library\, transmitter and receiver equipm
 ent\, and on-site staff support to develop a new transmission artwork. ELI
 GIBILITY: international\; collaborations allowed\; full-time students are 
 ineligible except rare post-graduate cases\; past residents and fellows ma
 y reapply\; applicants may apply to as many opportunities as they wish but
  will be offered at most one\; DJ sets are explicitly ineligible. A strong
  fit for signal-based\, broadcast\, spatial sound and radio-adjacent work.
  DEADLINE: 1 December 2026 at 11:59 pm EST. Finalists are interviewed by Z
 oom\, with notifications in late February 2027 and residencies during 2027
 .\n\nOrganisation: Wave Farm\nLocation: Wave Farm\, Acra\, NY\, US (Catski
 ll Mountain Park). International open call.\nAward: USD 1\,000 stipend for
  each 10-day residency\; USD 2\,000 for each two-month Radio Art Fellowshi
 p. No application fee.\nDuration: 10 days (Transmission Art and Spatial So
 und residencies) or 2 months (Radio Art Fellowships)\, during 2027.\n\nMor
 e: https://wavefarm.org/ta/residency-program/info-apply?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://wavefarm.org/ta/residency-program/info-apply?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Wave Farm\, Acra\, NY\, US (Catskill Mountain Park). International
  open call.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eyebeam-agency-virtual-residency-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260926T163109Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261011
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261012
SUMMARY:Deadline: Eyebeam AGENCY Virtual Residency\, Fall 2026 (US-based ar
 tists)
DESCRIPTION:Eyebeam's 2026-27 program year runs under the theme AGENCY: pre
 serving human agency as AI systems increasingly act\, decide and carry out
  tasks on our behalf\, asserting agency in imagining and building the soci
 eties we inhabit\, and rethinking agency as collective\, relational\, enta
 ngled and more-than-human. The Virtual Residency (Fall/Winter) supports US
 -based artists working outside New York City with time\, community and str
 ucture to develop new work around the theme\; it is well-suited to early-s
 tage projects where shared research\, discussion groups and feedback on na
 scent ideas would advance concept development and prototyping. Projects mi
 ght engage AI directly but do not have to\; Eyebeam welcomes critical and 
 creative approaches to power\, autonomy\, technological systems\, collecti
 ve action\, human and nonhuman relationships\, and the structures through 
 which agency is exercised\, including reimagining AI systems through colle
 ctive governance\, repair\, rehearsal or mischief\, and repurposing AI tow
 ard tools for the public good. STRUCTURE: six artists\, 12 weeks\, minimum
  3-4 hours per week\, with weekly cohort sessions\, independent project de
 velopment\, work-in-progress shares with peers and guest critics\, a closi
 ng virtual process presentation\, and a contribution to an end-of-year pub
 lication. ELIGIBILITY: individuals only (no collectives this cycle)\; not 
 enrolled in a full-time degree program\; not committed to another residenc
 y during the term\; US-based outside NYC for the full term. TIMELINE: dead
 line Sunday 11 October 2026\, 11:59 PM ET (no late applications)\; intervi
 ews 26-28 October\; notifications 29 October\; residency 4 November 2026 t
 o 12 February 2027. Info session Monday 28 September 2026\, 1 PM ET. Apply
  via Submittable\, free.\n\nOrganisation: Eyebeam\nLocation: Online. Appli
 cants must be based in the United States\, OUTSIDE New York City\, and rem
 ain US-based for the full residency term (4 November 2026 to 12 February 2
 027).\nAward: USD 2\,000 stipend for each of six selected artists\, plus w
 eekly residency programming\, mentorship with Eyebeam alumni and visiting 
 professionals\, technical consultation\, and access to tools and expertise
  through Eyebeam's network. No application fee (via Submittable).\nDuratio
 n: 12 weeks\, 4 November 2026 to 12 February 2027\; minimum commitment 3-4
  hours per week.\n\nMore: https://eyebeam.org/articles/agency-virtual-resi
 dency?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://eyebeam.org/articles/agency-virtual-residency?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online. Applicants must be based in the United States\, OUTSIDE Ne
 w York City\, and remain US-based for the full residency term (4 November 
 2026 to 12 February 2027).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eyebeam-agency-nyc-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260926T163109Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261115
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261116
SUMMARY:Deadline: Eyebeam AGENCY NYC Residency\, Spring 2027
DESCRIPTION:The NYC track of Eyebeam's AGENCY residency cycle supports New 
 York City-based artists exploring questions of agency at a moment when AI 
 systems increasingly act\, decide and carry out tasks on our behalf: how t
 o preserve human agency as these systems shape human behavior\, how to ass
 ert agency in imagining and building the societies we inhabit\, and what a
 gency becomes when understood as collective\, relational\, entangled and m
 ore-than-human. Projects might engage AI directly but do not have to\; cri
 tical and creative approaches to power\, autonomy\, technological systems\
 , collective action\, human and nonhuman relationships\, and the social an
 d material structures of agency are all welcome\, including reimagining AI
  through collective governance\, repair\, rehearsal or mischief\, and repu
 rposing AI systems toward new tools\, methods and models for the public go
 od. Residents receive a USD 5\,000 stipend\, mentorship\, and access to to
 ols and technical expertise through Eyebeam's network. ELIGIBILITY: indivi
 duals only (no collectives this cycle)\; not enrolled in a full-time degre
 e program\; not committed to another residency during the term\; must live
  in New York City within the five boroughs and be able to work on site at 
 Eyebeam at least one day a week for the full term. TIMELINE: deadline Sund
 ay 15 November 2026\, 11:59 PM ET\; interviews 7-10 December\; notificatio
 ns 14 December\; residency 15 March to 11 June 2027. Info session Monday 1
